  "_style": "Always address the user informally as “sina” (sina, sinu, sind, sulle; imperative 2nd person singular: „Salvesta“, „Proovi uuesti“), warm and short; address the couple together as „te“/„teie“ only when speaking to both (prefer „te mõlemad“, „teie kahe“, „koos“), never as a formal „Teie“. The other person is always „sinu partner“ / „partner“ (never poiss-sõber, tüdruksõber, mees, naine, kallim); Estonian has no grammatical gender, so use „tema“ for they/them and keep sentences gender-neutral. A partner's name placeholder (%@) is NEVER declined or given a case ending (no „%@-ga“, „%@ile“, „%@'i“): keep it in the nominative, e.g. as the subject („%@ saatis sulle kirja“) or as an apposition after a declined noun („partneriga %@“, „Kiri partnerilt %@“), or rephrase. Quotes: outer „ “ (low-high), inner ‚ ‘; use the single ellipsis character …; no space before . , : ; ! ?; keep the en/em dash with spaces as in the source, keep „ · “, emoji and \\n unchanged. Numbers: decimal comma (1,5), thousands with a non-breaking space (10 000), percent with a space (50 %); dates as 14.11.2026 or „14. november“, month and weekday names lowercase, 24-hour time 18:30. Buttons and actions are short imperatives without „palun“ („Salvesta“, „Pitseeri kiri“, „Kasta aeda“); titles, tabs, buttons and headers use sentence case (capital only on the first word), except proper names like Coupley, Premium, CoupleSync. Latin script with õ, ä, ö, ü, š, ž — never replace them with plain letters. Plural categories are [\"one\",\"other\"]: after a numeral Estonian uses the partitive singular, so „%lld päev“ (one) / „%lld päeva“ (other), never a plural noun after a number. Use „TI“ for AI (TI-nõustaja). Tone is warm, light and kind, never sugary; don't translate word for word.",
